   "marks": 5,
   "text": "The curve $C$ has equation $y = \\sqrt{x} + \\dfrac{8}{x^2}$, $x > 0$. The curve lies entirely above the $x$-axis.\n\nThe finite region $R$ is enclosed by $C$, the $x$-axis and the two vertical lines $x = 1$ and $x = 4$.\n\nUse algebraic integration to show that the exact area of $R$ is $\\dfrac{32}{3}$. Show each stage of your integration and your substitution of the limits. (5)",
   "command": "show-that",
   "skills": [
    "evaluate-definite-integral"
   ],
   "forms": [
    "exact"
   ],
   "mark_scheme": [
    {
     "code": "M1",
     "for": "Integrates the $\\sqrt{x}$ term by raising the power: $x^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\to k\\,x^{\\frac{3}{2}}$ for some non-zero constant $k$",
     "notes": "The index must become $\\frac{3}{2}$; the coefficient may be wrong for this mark"
    },
    {
     "code": "M1",
     "for": "Integrates the $\\frac{8}{x^2}$ term after writing it as $8x^{-2}$, obtaining $m\\,x^{-1}$ for some non-zero constant $m$",
     "notes": "Getting $x^{-3}$ or $x^{-2}$ scores M0"
    },
    {
     "code": "A1",
     "for": "Fully correct integral $\\frac{2}{3}x^{\\frac{3}{2}} - 8x^{-1}$",
     "notes": "oe, e.g. $\\frac{2}{3}x\\sqrt{x} - \\frac{8}{x}$; a $+c$ may be present or absent"
    },
    {
     "code": "M1",
     "for": "Puts $x = 4$ and $x = 1$ into their integrated expression and subtracts, value at 4 minus value at 1",
     "depends_on": "An integrated expression with at least one power increased or reduced as above",
     "notes": "Seeing $\\left(\\frac{16}{3} - 2\\right) - \\left(\\frac{2}{3} - 8\\right)$ or the equivalent from their integral is enough"
    },
    {
     "code": "A1*",
     "for": "Reaches $\\frac{10}{3} + \\frac{22}{3} = \\frac{32}{3}$ with no errors anywhere",
     "notes": "cso. The value at each limit, or the bracketed subtraction, has to be shown before the final $\\frac{32}{3}$. Using a calculator's integration function on its own scores 0"
    }
   ],
   "solution": [
    {
     "step": 1,
     "working": "Write the integrand as powers of $x$: $y = x^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 8x^{-2}$, so the area is $\\displaystyle\\int_1^4 \\left(x^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 8x^{-2}\\right)\\mathrm{d}x$.",
     "mark": "0"
    },
    {
     "step": 2,
     "working": "$\\displaystyle\\int x^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x = \\frac{x^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{\\frac{3}{2}} = \\frac{2}{3}x^{\\frac{3}{2}}$",
     "mark": "M1"
    },
    {
     "step": 3,
     "working": "$\\displaystyle\\int 8x^{-2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x = \\frac{8x^{-1}}{-1} = -8x^{-1}$",
     "mark": "M1"
    },
    {
     "step": 4,
     "working": "So $\\displaystyle\\int_1^4 \\left(x^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 8x^{-2}\\right)\\mathrm{d}x = \\left[\\frac{2}{3}x^{\\frac{3}{2}} - \\frac{8}{x}\\right]_1^4$",
     "mark": "A1"
    },
    {
     "step": 5,
     "working": "$= \\left(\\frac{2}{3}(8) - \\frac{8}{4}\\right) - \\left(\\frac{2}{3}(1) - \\frac{8}{1}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{16}{3} - 2\\right) - \\left(\\frac{2}{3} - 8\\right)$",
     "mark": "M1"
    },
    {
     "step": 6,
     "working": "$= \\frac{10}{3} - \\left(-\\frac{22}{3}\\right) = \\frac{32}{3}$. The region is above the $x$-axis, so the area of $R$ is $\\frac{32}{3}$, as required.",
     "mark": "A1*"
    }
   ],
   "answers": [
    {
     "name": "area",
     "expr": "32/3",
     "form": "exact"
    }
   ],
